# Deploying a Remote MCP Server

A remote MCP server is an HTTP service that speaks the Model Context Protocol. To deploy one, you build it with the **Streamable HTTP** transport, add authentication, host it at a public HTTPS URL, and give people that URL.

## 1. Use the right transport

- **Streamable HTTP** is the current standard. It uses a single endpoint, usually `/mcp`, that accepts POST requests and can stream responses.
- **HTTP+SSE** is the older transport and is deprecated. Support it only if you need to work with legacy clients.
- **stdio** works only for local servers, so it isn't an option here.

## 2. Build the server

Here is a minimal example using the TypeScript SDK (`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`) with Express:

```ts
import express from "express";
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { StreamableHTTPServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/streamableHttp.js";
import { z } from "zod";

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

function buildServer() {
  const server = new McpServer({ name: "my-server", version: "1.0.0" });
  server.tool("add", { a: z.number(), b: z.number() }, async ({ a, b }) => ({
    content: [{ type: "text", text: String(a + b) }],
  }));
  return server;
}

// Stateless mode: a fresh server and transport per request (easy to scale horizontally)
app.post("/mcp", async (req, res) => {
  const server = buildServer();
  const transport = new StreamableHTTPServerTransport({ sessionIdGenerator: undefined });
  res.on("close", () => { transport.close(); server.close(); });
  await server.connect(transport);
  await transport.handleRequest(req, res, req.body);
});

app.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000);
```

In Python, the official SDK's FastMCP does the same thing with `mcp.run(transport="streamable-http")`.

**Stateless vs. stateful:**
- Stateless mode is simplest and works well on serverless platforms.
- If you need sessions (`Mcp-Session-Id`), you'll have to use sticky routing or store session state somewhere shared.

## 3. Add authentication

If anyone besides you will connect, add authentication. The MCP authorization spec is based on **OAuth 2.1**:

- Your server acts as an OAuth **resource server**. It returns `401` with a `WWW-Authenticate` header and publishes **Protected Resource Metadata** at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` (RFC 9728). That metadata points to your authorization server.
- The **authorization server** can be your own or a provider such as Auth0, WorkOS, Stytch, Clerk, Cognito, or Keycloak. It should support PKCE, and ideally Dynamic Client Registration (or Client ID Metadata Documents), so clients like Claude can register themselves automatically.
- Validate the bearer token on every request, including its audience. Don't pass tokens through to upstream APIs.

For an internal or private server, a static API key in a header is a simpler option. Not every client supports custom headers, though.

## 4. Pick a host

| Option | Notes |
|---|---|
| **Cloudflare Workers** | Has first-class MCP support (`agents` SDK / `McpAgent`) and an OAuth provider library. Run `npm create cloudflare@latest -- my-mcp --template=cloudflare/ai/demos/remote-mcp-authless` to start. |
| **Vercel / Netlify** | Vercel's `mcp-handler` adapter works with Next.js or plain functions. |
| **Containers** (Cloud Run, Fly.io, Railway, Render, ECS) | Deploy the Express or FastAPI app above as a Docker image. |
| **Your own VM** | Put it behind a reverse proxy (nginx or Caddy) with TLS. |

On any host, check these:
- **HTTPS is required.**
- **Disable response buffering** on proxies so streaming works. In nginx, use `proxy_buffering off;`.
- **Set long enough timeouts** for slow tool calls.
- **Validate the `Origin` header** to prevent DNS rebinding, and configure CORS if browser-based clients will connect.

## 5. Test it

- **MCP Inspector:** run `npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector`, choose "Streamable HTTP", and enter `https://your-host/mcp`.
- Exercise the full OAuth flow end to end before you share the server.

## 6. How others connect

Share the URL, for example `https://your-host.example.com/mcp`. Then:

- **Claude.ai / Claude Desktop:** go to Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector and paste the URL. On Team and Enterprise plans, an admin can add it for the whole org.
- **Claude Code:** run `claude mcp add --transport http my-server https://your-host.example.com/mcp`, then run `/mcp` to authenticate.
- **Claude API:** use the MCP connector by passing `mcp_servers: [{ type: "url", url, name, authorization_token }]`.
- **Other clients** (Cursor, VS Code, etc.): add the URL in their MCP config.

## Production checklist

- Per-user authorization and scoped tokens
- Rate limiting and logging
- Input validation on tool arguments
- Clear tool descriptions, which help the model use your tools correctly
- Versioning, so changes don't break existing users

